
This is a query performance degradation problem — the query works fine at small scale but degrades as data grows. This is almost always caused by one or more of:
Full table scan → Missing indexes → N+1 queries → Bad query structure → No pagination → ORM misuse
The approach is: Measure first, assume nothing, fix what data tells you.

Diagnosis first
 step 1 - Get the actual query
          Enable SQL logging ang get the actual query that is hitting the DB
 step 2 - Run Explain
          Read the output - red flags
          ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
          │ type = ALL    → FULL TABLE SCAN ❌ biggest red flag      │
          │ type = index  → full index scan (okay)                  │
          │ type = ref    → index lookup ✅                         │
          │ type = const  → single row lookup ✅✅ best             │
          │                                                         │
          │ rows = 20000  → scanning ALL rows ❌                    │
          │ rows = 5      → only 5 rows scanned ✅                  │
          │                                                         │
          │ Extra = Using filesort    → no index for ORDER BY ❌    │
          │ Extra = Using temporary   → temp table created ❌       │
          │ Extra = Using index       → covered index ✅✅          │
          └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

 Step 3 Identify the root cause
        Problem 1 missing index
                -- Query filtering on unindexed column
                SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'PENDING';
                -- DB scans ALL 20K rows to find PENDING ones ❌

                -- Fix: Add index
                CREATE INDEX idx_orders_status ON orders(status);

                -- Composite index if filtering on multiple columns
                CREATE INDEX idx_orders_status_created
                ON orders(status, created_at DESC);
        problem 2 N+1 query
                // The silent killer — looks fine in code, disaster in DB

                // You fetch 20K orders
                List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findAll(); // 1 query

                for (Order order : orders) {
                    order.getUser().getName(); // triggers 1 query PER order
                }
                // Result: 1 + 20,000 = 20,001 queries fired ❌💥
                this can be detected in logs
                In logs you'll see the SAME query repeated thousands of times:
                SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?   [id=1]
                SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?   [id=2]
                SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?   [id=3]
                ... (20,000 times)

                Fix
                // Fix 1: JPQL JOIN FETCH
                @Query("SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.user WHERE o.status = :status")
                List<Order> findWithUser(@Param("status") String status);
                // Result: 1 query with JOIN ✅

                // Fix 2: @EntityGraph
                @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"user", "items"})
                List<Order> findByStatus(String status);

                // Fix 3: Batch size (partial fix)
                @BatchSize(size = 100) // fetches 100 users in one IN query
                private User user;
        problem 3: No pagination, all 20K records fetched at once
                // Fetching everything into memory ❌
                List<Order> orders = orderRepo.findAll(); // loads 20K objects into heap

                // Fix: Always paginate
                Page<Order> orders = orderRepo.findAll(
                    PageRequest.of(0, 20, Sort.by("createdAt").descending())
                );
                // Only 20 records fetched and loaded ✅
                -- SQL level
                SELECT * FROM orders
                ORDER BY created_at DESC
                LIMIT 20 OFFSET 0;
        problem 4: Select * (fetching unnecessary columns)
                  fix fetch only necessary columns
        problem 5 : Function on Indexed Column (Index Bypass)

        Situation: Order dashboard was loading in 200ms with 5K records.
        After 3 months, data grew to 20K → response time jumped to 8 seconds.

        Step 1: Enabled SQL logging → spotted N+1 on Order→User→Address chain
                1 order fetch + 20K user queries + 20K address queries = 40,001 queries

        Step 2: EXPLAIN showed type=ALL on orders.status column

        Step 3: Fixes applied:
                → Added composite index on (status, created_at)
                → Changed findAll() to JOIN FETCH with pagination
                → Used DTO projection instead of full entity

        Result: 8 seconds → 180ms ✅